      "content": "Research on biological differences in pain perception among racial or ethnic groups suggests that some studies have found variations in pain sensitivity, but these findings are not well-consolidated or universally accepted. Most clinical organizations and pain centers conclude that individual differences in pain perception are more closely related to genetic predisposition, life experiences, and psychological factors than to innate biological differences associated with racial or ethnic background. The American Academy of Pain Medicine, for example, does not recognize racial or ethnic differences in pain perception as a valid clinical observation.",
